<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/advanced/entrepreneurship-and-venture-funding">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Entrepreneurship is <strong>searching for a repeatable, scalable business model under extreme
    uncertainty</strong> (Blank's definition) — which makes it a discipline of cheap experiments
    rather than confident plans. Venture funding is the fuel for that search: staged capital that
    buys the next round of experiments, in exchange for equity and board influence.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="problem, loop, runway, terms" widget="equationStep" config={entCfg} />

  <h2>The survival statistics, honestly</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>~90% of startups fail;</strong> of VC-funded ones, most return little — venture economics is a power law where a few winners pay for the portfolio. Founders should model for success but budget for the base rates.</li>
    <li><strong>Founder equity is precious:</strong> every round dilutes; options pools, preferences and down-rounds compound. The cap table is the founder's balance sheet — model it forward.</li>
    <li><strong>Cofounder conflict kills more startups than competitors:</strong> Noam Wasserman's "Founder's Dilemmas" research — clear equity splits (vesting!) and role boundaries decided early prevent the classic dissolution.</li>
    <li><strong>The alternative paths are real:</strong> bootstrapping (own the company, grow slower — Basecamp), revenue-based finance, small buyouts. Venture capital fits few businesses; the myth that it fits all is venture marketing.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ries, The Lean Startup; Blank, "Why the Lean Start-Up Changes Everything" (HBR 2013); Wasserman, The Founder's Dilemmas.</p>
</TopicPage>
